"""Silverpine dungeons: one story speech, and the outer direction the old accept line used to give."""

STORY = {
    'sfk_annotated_page': ("The page came off a Moonwalker, and Arugal didn't write it. It's a survey of a keep, drawn in a neat court hand, with the inner ward measured and the garrison strength noted in the margin. The keep isn't Shadowfang. Somebody in Alterac is paying for surveys of somebody else's walls, and somebody in Shadowfang was carrying one. There's a Forsaken scholar watching a keep in Hillsbrad from the bluff above Durnholde, Scholar Emeline Vask, and she'll know exactly what she's looking at.", 'You roll the page carefully. The margin notes are the part somebody will want to read.'),
    'spn_wl_silka': ("The hive buds off a single queen, and her name is Silka. She's in the west lair, off the Web Heart.\n\nHere's what that means, and it's why I'm sending you at her rather than at the numbers. Everything Longleg ever was came out of her eggs. Every spider in those woods that anybody has ever lost a dog or a child to comes from one line, from one source, and the source is still laying.\n\nCorvane assumed for years that the woods simply had spiders, the way woods do. Then he counted, and his count only works if there's one.\n\nEnd the line. Follow the egg sacs west. They get thicker the closer you are. She's what they're all running from, and Corvane's watched them do it. The Widow's Lair is east of here.", None),
    'spn_wl_velaxis': ("Above the Web Heart there's a spire, and Velaxis nests in it.\n\nShe's older than Silka and larger than Skitter, and she isn't fond of company, which in anything else I'd call a personality and in her I'd call a warning.\n\nShe's the head of this hive. Silka was the source and Skitter was the keeper, and both of them answered upward. Corvane didn't believe spiders did that until he spent a season at the mouth of that place watching them. Cut her off, and the hive is a lot of legs with nothing arranging them.\n\nGo up the north neck from the Web Heart. The Spire is lit by her eyes, and that's how you'll know you've arrived. I won't pretend this is anything but a death sentence for whoever tries it. The Widow's Lair is east of here.", None),
    'spn_wl_venom': ("I'm Apothecary Pell. Don't lean over the cauldron. It's at a delicate stage, and so am I.\n\nMy venom theory has acquired a new requirement, which is what theories do when they're going well. I need stalker venom. The Silverpine spider glands I've been using aren't strong enough, by a good half, which I established the tedious way over four months and eleven batches.\n\nThe Venom Stalkers inside the Lair have what I want. Cut the sac at the base, seal it, and then run, in that order, because two of my suppliers reversed the last two.\n\nAnd please don't open one to look. Everybody wants to look. It's green, it's famous, and the one who looked is why I now say this to everybody. The Widow's Lair is south-east of here.", None),
    'sps_at_reagents': ("The Alchemy Lab was Dalaran's best-stocked apothecary outside the Violet Citadel. That's an enormous amount of material in one room, sealed and sitting there.\n\nAnd its alchemists never stopped brewing. They've done it for decades, in a sealed room, with no one taking anything out. I'm a millwright, and even I can see where that ends. Every batch they finish has to go somewhere, and it's been going into the floor, and the floor is above the lower hall.\n\nI want sealed vials out of there before the slow poison gets properly out of hand. I want enough for somebody to say what's in the floor.\n\nThe Forsaken Alchemists carry them on their belts, north-east of the lower hall. Follow the smell, and don't breathe it. Ambermill Tower is south-east of here.", None),
    'sps_at_helbrim': ("Apothecary Helbrim runs that lab, if running is the word for a man who's been brewing the same batch for decades. I've decided it isn't, and I haven't found the right one.\n\nHe calls it research. I've thought about that word a great deal since you brought me his ledger, because I used to call what I did at the mill engineering, and the two are closer than I'd like. I stopped when it killed somebody, and he didn't. He initialled an order for more and dated it the same week.\n\nSo it's murder. That's my word for it, and I'd use it to his face if I ever got the chance, which I won't, which is why I'm asking you.\n\nGo to the Alchemy Lab and follow the smell. And wear something you'd be happy to burn afterward. Ambermill Tower is south-east of here.", None),
    'sps_at_atiesh': ("The tower's last occupant is Archmage Atiesh, and he keeps the Observatory, which is where the waygate was aimed.\n\nNobody at Ambermill has put those two facts side by side, and the moment you do, the whole building reorganises itself around them. That's an observatory with a tower under it, and everything beneath, the reading hall, the lab, the lecterns, Hawren, was support. Somebody was looking at something for a very long time, and the waygate was how the looking got out.\n\nI want to know what he was aiming at. And I'll say this plainly: I hope the answer dies with him.\n\nGo to the very top. The stair is long. Don't stop on the landings, not to rest and not to look. The landings are where it talks to you. Ambermill Tower is south-east of here.", None),
    'sps_sfk_rethilgore': ("Rethilgore keeps the hounds in Shadowfang, and he keeps them hungry on purpose. A fed hound sleeps, and a hungry one watches the door.\n\nHe was a decent man in Pyrewood. Brand knew him, and so did half the village. I've watched him through a glass, and he's still careful. He checks the chains and he counts the hounds. There's enough of him left to do the job properly, and not enough to stop doing it.\n\nI'd like him buried. He's in the big pen in the west wing, and he'll have a chain in his hand. Shadowfang Keep is south of here.", None),
    'sps_sfk_razorclaw': ("My name's Pell. Marl keeps the Shadowfang gate, and I take his nights.\n\nThe Butcher's Yard is east of the bailey, and Razorclaw keeps it. He's a worgen with a cleaver where a hand ought to be. He doesn't hold one, he has one instead.\n\nMarl's heard the chopping from this gate for years, and I've heard it every night watch since I got here. It's regular, and it isn't a frenzy or a fight. It's the sound of a man working, at a steady pace, for hours, and then stopping, and then starting again the next day. Something is being prepared in that yard on a schedule, and the schedule hasn't changed in all the time Marl's stood at that gate.\n\nFollow the sound. You'll hear it from the gatehouse. Shadowfang Keep is south of here.", None),
    'sps_sfk_guards': ("I'm Ashe, the field surgeon.\n\nThe keep's old guard never left their posts. The guardsmen wail where they were posted, which I know because the positions make sense. Stand in the Great Hall and you can read the old watch order off them. And the officers keep giving orders, to no one, out loud, in the proper form, with pauses where the acknowledgement should go.\n\nThey were never relieved. Somebody should have come to take that watch, and nobody ever did, and they're all still standing there waiting for it.\n\nSo relieve them, one way or another. Take the officers first, then the men. That's the order it should have happened in, and it's the only courtesy left to pay them. Shadowfang Keep is south of here.", None),
    'sps_sfk_keys': ("The haunted servitors still carry the keep's keys on their belts, a full ring each. They aren't guarding the doors. They're simply still carrying what they carried, because that's what a servitor does and nobody ever took them back.\n\nRight now the cloisters and the armoury are a gamble. You come to a door in that keep, you don't know if it's locked, and you find out by standing in front of it long enough for something to arrive. I've stitched up what that costs.\n\nLook in the Great Hall and the cloisters. Take the ring, not the key. I want whole rings, because a ring tells you which doors went together, and that's a floor plan, and nobody's ever had one of those. Shadowfang Keep is south of here.", None),
    'sps_sfk_silverlaine': ("Baron Silverlaine owned Pyrewood, and Shadowfang, and half the pines between. Most of the people who worked them never saw him.\n\nHe sits on his dais in the Great Hall and receives no one, and that's exactly what he did alive. Nothing about his conduct has changed. He held court and received nobody, and he does the same now. The only difference is that now there's genuinely nobody to receive.\n\nI'd prefer the hall empty. I don't want him avenged or reckoned with. As long as he's sitting at the far end of it, that keep still has an owner, and a keep with an owner is a place other things come to serve in.\n\nHe's at the far end of the Great Hall. He doesn't stand. He doesn't need to, and he never has. Shadowfang Keep is south of here.", None),
    'sps_sfk_moonwalkers': ("I keep the night watch on the Shadowfang gate, and on a full moon you can hear the keep chanting from here. That's the moonwalkers. Arugal's cult is half sorcerers and half worgen who are still turning, and the chanting is how they get the rest of the way.\n\nThe darksouls are what's at the end of it. So the keep isn't a garrison with casters in it. The casters are making the garrison, out of whatever walks in.\n\nGo in wherever you hear chanting, and kill the Shadowfang Moonwalkers and the Shadowfang Darksouls. Kill the casters first, every time. Shadowfang Keep is south of here.", None),
    'sps_sfk_fenrus': ("Fenrus was Arugal's first experiment and his least successful. I've had that from two sources, and neither of them was being kind about it. Least successful means it didn't do what he wanted. It's very much alive down there.\n\nHe devours things in the crypt, and nothing that goes in comes out. That's a tally, not drama. Marl's kept the gate nineteen years, and he knows what's gone down that cloister and what hasn't come back up it, and the two numbers are the same number.\n\nMarl and I would like, once, for something to come back out of that hole on its own feet.\n\nHe's at the end of the cloister. And mind the sarcophagi on the way down. Three people have told me they breathe, and I've stopped asking them to explain it. Shadowfang Keep is south of here.", None),
    'sps_sfk_sons': ("The inner keep is stocked with Arugal's best, and I use stocked with intent. There are Sons of Arugal in half-turned shapes, and Lupine Horrors that don't fit through the doors they're standing behind. And there are Deathsworn Captains, who command them.\n\nThat last part is why I won't call this a pack. A pack sorts itself out. It has a top and a bottom, and every animal in it knows where it stands without anybody writing it down. This has officers. Something decided who commands whom, and that's a cult with fangs, and a cult doesn't break when you kill the biggest one.\n\nClear the crypt, the armoury and the upper landing. Don't fight them in a corridor. That's where the Horrors are useful, and they know it. Shadowfang Keep is south of here.", None),
    'sps_sfk_arugal': ("Archmage Arugal opened the door that let the worgen into Lordaeron.\n\nEverybody knows that sentence, and almost nobody follows it through. He opened a door, as a measure against the Scourge, and it worked, briefly, and then it kept being open. Everything in this valley since has come out of it: the hills, the keep, Pyrewood, Rynn's whole trade. And he sits at the top of this keep with his tower of shadow, and he seems to think it went well.\n\nI've stood at his gate nineteen years. I'd like to see the other side of it.\n\nGo to the upper landing, then the Sanctum. Don't stop, and don't answer when he speaks. He will, and it'll sound like a question you'd want to answer. Shadowfang Keep is south of here.", None),
}
